$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{7}{25})(-\\frac{4}{5})+(-\\frac{24}{25})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{125}-\\frac{72}{125} \\\\  & =-\\frac{44}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184839 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184839 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74457" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184839 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184839 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{15}{17}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184839 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f39d2c0-c3fb-5d82-bb15-c8596eec6f87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184839 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{15}{17})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{15}^{2}}{{17}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{225}{289} \\\\  & =\\frac{289}{289}-\\frac{225}{289} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{289}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{64}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{8}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{8}{17}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{15}{17})+(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{8}{17}) \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{85}+\\frac{24}{85} \\\\  & =\\frac{84}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184893 #edb/ref 1061 11781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184893 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ac838c11-0bfd-54e8-b4e8-850cec75401e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184893 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185023 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186185024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185023 #edb/ref 1061 37640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185023 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c28e4265-3725-531e-aa72-0bd495eb57af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185023 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185024 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185024 #edb/ref 1061 37641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185024 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9c9e3456-ffce-525d-a541-0be82ec5af0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185024 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185350 #edb/ref 1064 "We can compute trigonometric ratios using the sum and difference formulas for cosine together with the Pythagorean identity.\n\nLet's suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u = \\dfrac{1}{3}, \\qquad\n\\sin v = \\dfrac {3}{5}, \\qquad\n\\cos v = -\\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nHow can we find the value of $\\cos\\left(u - v\\right)$ given that $u$ lies in the second quadrant?\n\nFirst, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\sin{v}$, and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u = 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\cos(u - v) \n\t\t\t&= \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\left( -\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\right) \\left(-\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right) +  \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right) \\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right) \\[5pt]\t\t\t\n \t\t\t&= \\dfrac{8\\sqrt{2}}{15} + \\dfrac{3}{15} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{8\\sqrt{2}+3}{15}\n\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185350 #edb/ref 1065 6209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185350 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "75e177a6-4974