 we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{7}{25},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{24}{25},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that both $u$ and $v$ lie in the first quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{3}\\,+\\,$ {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $50$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552407 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1059df0b-e279-5183-9a0b-f6c9c0f186a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552407 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive, we take\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{24}{25})(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+(\\frac{7}{25})(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\sqrt{3}}{50}+\\frac{7}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\sqrt{3}+7}{50}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552416 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552416 #edb/ref 1069 "q-119581" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552416 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552416 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{5}{13},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{3}-b}{26},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552416 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ed725f0-66d2-5a2d-ad80-26e42df0423f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552416 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡u=1-{cos}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡u & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{5}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{12}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{13})(-\\frac{1}{2})-(\\frac{12}{13})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{26}+\\frac{12\\sqrt{3}}{26} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\sqrt{3}-5}{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=12,b=5,$ and $a+b=17.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552427 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552427 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141440" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552427 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552427 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{5}{13}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{7}-b\\sqrt{2}}{39},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552427 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f0420a1-666b-5191-842f-3d2bdb44e681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552427 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{5}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{12}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\opera